    ('2e0236e5-ca62-49f1-8532-303463aba86b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Notgrove Long Barrow is a prehistoric long barrow burial mound in Gloucestershire.', 'In Gloucestershire, a passage runs through the heart of Notgrove Long Barrow to small chambers where human remains were laid. Open for thousands of years, it was sealed in 1976.', 'teaser-card-s012:2e0236e5-ca62-49f1-8532-303463aba86b:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'edc61ad6a1f3ffe6d5cf70505c73d8cfe474e04d2111941ac4915b4b2d4ef411|128f4fe48017d6bbed4ec48c1b1c87445d1399063390a20b0ed6e30c582216bd|128f4fe48017d6bbed4ec48c1b1c87445d1399063390a20b0ed6e30c582216bd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Gloucestershire, a passage runs through the heart of Notgrove Long Barrow to small chambers where human remains were laid. Open for thousands of years, it was sealed in 1976."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Notgrove Long Barrow is in Gloucestershire [S1]; it is a long barrow [S1]; a passage runs through its centre [S2]; small chambers open off the passage [S2]; human remains were interred in the chambers [S3]; the barrow was open for thousands of years [S4]; it was sealed in 1976 [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Notgrove Long Barrow is a long barrow (a prehistoric burial mound) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; Notgrove Long Barrow is in Gloucestershire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; A passage runs through the heart (centre) of the barrow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; The passage leads to small chambers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; Human remains were laid (interred) in the chambers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; The barrow was open for thousands of years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; The barrow was sealed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]; The barrow was sealed in 1976 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Notgrove_Long_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2fecf287-b758-45e4-bc5c-81c6d1196f2f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman temple on the highest point of ancient Barcino. Four Corinthian columns still stand in a medieval courtyard, hidden in the Gothic Quarter.', 'The central building on Tàber Hill, in what is now the Gothic Quarter, was the Temple of Augustus, a Roman temple raised in the Imperial period in the colony of Barcino.', 'teaser-card-s012:2fecf287-b758-45e4-bc5c-81c6d1196f2f:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 169 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '19fc252bbc0a663704957d8a72efba5e3982d14eb3fd6d970efb2477af8ab1c9|6d50d919d820ba6a2a3dbe6f613d374932778ad8d1673b619553351d24fd5300|6d50d919d820ba6a2a3dbe6f613d374932778ad8d1673b619553351d24fd5300', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The central building on Tàber Hill, in what is now the Gothic Quarter, was the Temple of Augustus, a Roman temple raised in the Imperial period in the colony of Barcino."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Temple of Augustus was the central building on Tàber Hill [S2]; Tàber Hill lies in the Gothic Quarter [S2]; it was a Roman temple [S1]; it was built during the Imperial period [S1]; it stood in the colony of Barcino [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The site is a Roman temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; It was built in the Imperial period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; It stood in the colony of Barcino (modern Barcelona) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; The temple was the central building on Tàber Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; Tàber Hill lies in what is now Barcelona''s Gothic Quarter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; The building is known as the Temple of Augustus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Augustus,_Barcelona]; The temple was dedicated to Augustus (the source calls the dedication traditional but unproven) [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('31189b26-92cd-4486-a696-35cc37daf70b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Romano-Celtic temple at the center of a vast sanctuary. Its site has Neolithic roots but was monumentally rebuilt under Roman rule.', 'Two walls of its cella still rise over 20 meters in Autun, yet the deity once worshipped in the Temple of Janus is unknown: the name rests on no archaeological or historic fact.', 'teaser-card-s012:31189b26-92cd-4486-a696-35cc37daf70b:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '80f4eef344d11b0f8b7c0843d44f151527d3dc118f4438cc013f7e9b17fd8be3|a1a8f665adbcf658b08eab0795fc94203cea52ae5eb0cf91d4b800daa1469646|a1a8f665adbcf658b08eab0795fc94203cea52ae5eb0cf91d4b800daa1469646', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Two walls of its cella still rise over 20 meters in Autun, yet the deity once worshipped in the Temple of Janus is unknown: the name rests on no archaeological or historic fact."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Temple of Janus is in Autun [S1]; two sides of its cella survive [S4]; they still rise over 20 meters [S4]; the deity once worshipped in the temple is unknown [S5]; its supposed dedication to Janus, which gives it its name, rests on no archaeological or historic fact [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The site is a temple (a Romano-Celtic religious structure) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]; It is located in Autun (France) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]; Two walls (sides) of its cella still stand [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]; The surviving walls of the cella rise over 20 meters [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]; The deity once worshipped in the temple is unknown [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]; The name Temple of Janus rests on no archaeological or historic fact [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Janus_(Autun)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3195c970-3603-4a2c-9810-7031b5120420'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The only archaeological reserve in the country, home to hunebedden—megalithic tombs built from glacial boulders. Over 50 survive in this province alone.', 'Between Anloo and Schipborg, 377 hectares of Drenthe keep two dolmens and some sixty burial mounds: Strubben-Kniphorstbos, the only archaeological reserve in the Netherlands.', 'teaser-card-s012:3195c970-3603-4a2c-9810-7031b5120420:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 174 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '10ceb1796a1c61f311c52dec6f39b132dedd954f3703014fbc68659820403977|64613ee5d1c86213d5ce5141d2c5c03f02e08bdf7bc600274333398f8c86bf51|64613ee5d1c86213d5ce5141d2c5c03f02e08bdf7bc600274333398f8c86bf51', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Between Anloo and Schipborg, 377 hectares of Drenthe keep two dolmens and some sixty burial mounds: Strubben-Kniphorstbos, the only archaeological reserve in the Netherlands."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Strubben-Kniphorstbos lies between Anloo and Schipborg [S1]; it covers 377 hectares [S1]; it is in Drenthe [S1]; it contains two dolmens [S3]; it contains some sixty burial mounds [S4]; it is the only archaeological reserve in the Netherlands [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Strubben-Kniphorstbos is a nature/archaeological reserve in the Dutch province of Drenthe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; It lies between Anloo and Schipborg [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; It covers 377 hectares [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; Two dolmens (D7 and D8) lie within the reserve [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; The reserve contains some sixty burial mounds [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; It is the only archaeological reserve in the Netherlands [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strubben%E2%80%93Kniphorstbos]; It is the only archaeological reserve in the Netherlands (Dutch Wikipedia) [SUPPORTED: https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Strubben-Kniphorstbos]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('32124d0d-d0af-4613-a3f1-b1e5f1a14f0c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Declared a national monument in 1912, this 1st-century BC aqueduct in Mérida channelled water to Emerita Augusta. Its arches follow the valley terrain north of the city centre.', 'Built in the first century to carry water into Emerita Augusta, capital of Roman Lusitania, the San Lázaro Roman Aqueduct now belongs to a World Heritage Site in present-day Mérida.', 'teaser-card-s012:32124d0d-d0af-4613-a3f1-b1e5f1a14f0c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8ecc5095eb8289bb21ce868369aff07fd41ee71533803f9c128fc422bab19531|aed1068a21b9422751cd51c92e859b88e8c88f93fe6bb88e6530a7c07e5f55b4|aed1068a21b9422751cd51c92e859b88e8c88f93fe6bb88e6530a7c07e5f55b4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built in the first century to carry water into Emerita Augusta, capital of Roman Lusitania, the San Lázaro Roman Aqueduct now belongs to a World Heritage Site in present-day Mérida."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was built in the first century [S2]; it was built to carry water into the city [S2]; the city is Emerita Augusta [S1]; Emerita Augusta was the capital of Roman Lusitania [S1]; it is a Roman aqueduct called San Lázaro [S1]; it now belongs to a World Heritage Site [S4]; it lies in present-day Mérida [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The San Lázaro Roman Aqueduct is a Roman aqueduct (a water-supply structure) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; It lies in present-day Mérida, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; It was built in the first century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; It was built to carry water into the city of Emerita Augusta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; The city the aqueduct served was Emerita Augusta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; Emerita Augusta was the capital of Roman Lusitania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; The aqueduct belongs to the Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]; The Archaeological Ensemble of Mérida is a World Heritage Site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_L%C3%A1zaro_Roman_aqueduct]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('32f8606a-075b-4e6c-afae-427a49ef9df4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 4th-millennium BC chambered tomb built from local sarsen megaliths. It was dismantled when found in 1822 and the human remains inside were scattered.', 'Discovered in 1822 and then dismantled, Smythe''s Megalith was a long barrow of earth and sarsen stones near Aylesford in Kent, where human remains were laid in its stone chamber.', 'teaser-card-s012:32f8606a-075b-4e6c-afae-427a49ef9df4:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 178 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '304bd096ac67f7b52c1a15cee812e15bf31b37e6e82ab790eadf9a49fddad0d8|6209d9d56ace69fe437fc587301c7cd36f22f416c7d89b380de923857a89c55e|6209d9d56ace69fe437fc587301c7cd36f22f416c7d89b380de923857a89c55e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Discovered in 1822 and then dismantled, Smythe''s Megalith was a long barrow of earth and sarsen stones near Aylesford in Kent, where human remains were laid in its stone chamber."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was discovered in 1822 [S2]; it was dismantled after its discovery [S2]; it was a long barrow [S1, S3]; it was built of earth and sarsen stones [S3]; it lay near Aylesford [S1]; it lay in Kent [S1]; it had a stone chamber [S3]; human remains were deposited in its stone chamber [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Smythe''s Megalith was a long barrow (a chambered long barrow) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It stood near Aylesford [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; Aylesford and the barrow are in Kent (England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It was discovered in 1822 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It was dismantled after its discovery (it no longer stands) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It was built partly of earth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It was built partly of sarsen stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; It had a stone chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]; Human remains were laid in its stone chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smythe%27s_Megalith]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3329c7e1-5714-4e5f-b101-21c35f905e9e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'One of the most ancient cities of Greece in Phocis, 2nd-1st millennium BC. Per the Homeric Hymn to Apollo, Crissa was founded by Cretans led by Apollo — chosen to be his priests at Pytho.', 'The Iliad hailed divine Crissa, a town on a spur of Mount Parnassus near Delphi, counted among the most ancient cities in Greece. Its fate is uncertain, yet its polygonal walls remained.', 'teaser-card-s012:3329c7e1-5714-4e5f-b101-21c35f905e9e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'a2fec1f74a61673859153a7c854bfa9651225da0d9e3237f9d707e89f6a88bdd|33517ccd88620a4c6c6f9a9cb9ec49f5cdb4782a9d02acae5d48b795b0289b3f|33517ccd88620a4c6c6f9a9cb9ec49f5cdb4782a9d02acae5d48b795b0289b3f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Iliad hailed divine Crissa, a town on a spur of Mount Parnassus near Delphi, counted among the most ancient cities in Greece. Its fate is uncertain, yet its polygonal walls remained."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Iliad names it divine Crissa [S4]; Crissa was a town [S1]; it lay on a spur of Mount Parnassus [S3]; it lay near Delphi [S3]; it was regarded as one of the most ancient cities in Greece [S2]; its fate is uncertain [S6]; its polygonal walls remained standing [S7, S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Crissa was a town of ancient Greece (in Phocis) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; The Iliad mentions Crissa as divine (\"divine Crissa\") [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; Crissa lay on a spur of Mount Parnassus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; Crissa was near Delphi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; Crissa was counted among the most ancient cities in Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; Crissa''s fate is uncertain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]; Polygonal walls of Crissa remained (visible ruins) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crissa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34139009-7bb5-42d7-b9d1-f5163fdb856d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An Iron Age earthwork from 800 BC running for several kilometres. Linear earthworks like this marked tribal boundaries — not walls for defense, but lines of territorial claim.', 'Once thought a Roman road, Aves Ditch is an Iron Age boundary running about 3 miles through Oxfordshire that may have divided the Dobunni from the Catuvellauni. A skull lay in its ditch.', 'teaser-card-s012:34139009-7bb5-42d7-b9d1-f5163fdb856d:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '575977d200a8635a18c27da304f37a354a993ba7bf43fff5f92e3e5b5c0631c7|4f39247f49f2a94b1547a62bd28f653d91b778df07e1a77d7bcb08ad4e8b7130|4f39247f49f2a94b1547a62bd28f653d91b778df07e1a77d7bcb08ad4e8b7130', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Once thought a Roman road, Aves Ditch is an Iron Age boundary running about 3 miles through Oxfordshire that may have divided the Dobunni from the Catuvellauni. A skull lay in its ditch."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was once believed to be a Roman road [S2]; it is an Iron Age boundary [S1, S2]; it runs about 3 miles [S1]; it runs through Oxfordshire [S1]; it may have formed the boundary between the Dobunni and the Catuvellauni [S4]; a skull was found in its ditch [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Aves Ditch is a boundary (a ditch and bank structure / boundary dyke) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; Aves Ditch dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; It was once thought to be a Roman road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; It runs about 3 miles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; It runs through Oxfordshire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; It may have divided the Dobunni from the Catuvellauni [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]; A skull lay in its ditch [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aves_ditch]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('349e080c-0b4f-4708-8744-d1b1c6a0b83e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The temple, built in the second quarter of the fifth century BC, was the very model of the fully developed classical Greek temple of the Doric order.', 'At Olympia, the Temple of Zeus housed a gold and ivory statue of the god, approximately 13 m high and one of the Seven Wonders, while its metopes told the twelve labours of Heracles.', 'teaser-card-s012:349e080c-0b4f-4708-8744-d1b1c6a0b83e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '05e033569bff41a1f18efa03be170005436d909ad8fdfa0198c362cadd84fa49|97295bc578eb0d52eec827a6935b784c58f26efa24209c81b70954f91a1bb270|97295bc578eb0d52eec827a6935b784c58f26efa24209c81b70954f91a1bb270', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Olympia, the Temple of Zeus housed a gold and ivory statue of the god, approximately 13 m high and one of the Seven Wonders, while its metopes told the twelve labours of Heracles."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Temple of Zeus stood at Olympia [S1]; the temple housed a statue of Zeus [S7]; the statue was made of gold and ivory [S8]; the statue was approximately 13 m high [S8]; the statue was one of the Seven Wonders [S7]; the temple''s metopes depict the twelve labours of Heracles [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Temple of Zeus is an ancient Greek temple dedicated to Zeus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; It stood at Olympia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; The temple housed a statue of Zeus (the god) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; The statue was of gold and ivory [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; The statue was approximately 13 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; The statue was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]; The temple''s metopes depicted the twelve labours of Heracles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Zeus,_Olympia]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('34f757be-a065-458c-9200-05b3beeeca2c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'c. 1500 BC Bronze Age cemetery of at least 11 round barrows south of Grantham. Five were quarried away in 1959; the remaining six are scheduled as ancient monuments in Lincolnshire.', 'On high ground south of Grantham lie the round barrows of the Ponton Heath Barrow Cemetery. In Barrow J, a crushed red urn decorated with cord impressions sat inverted over burned bone.', 'teaser-card-s012:34f757be-a065-458c-9200-05b3beeeca2c:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 185 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '0d826ef8e30515071a635c77e3bf22c49918e246f5a2f10adcfa487de0b06d45|558df7cebb71681767218247154781788ca5ee1cc1f6e1e1573d0243e4620fd1|558df7cebb71681767218247154781788ca5ee1cc1f6e1e1573d0243e4620fd1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On high ground south of Grantham lie the round barrows of the Ponton Heath Barrow Cemetery. In Barrow J, a crushed red urn decorated with cord impressions sat inverted over burned bone."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the cemetery lies on high ground [S3]; it lies south of Grantham [S1, S3]; it consists of round barrows [S1]; there is a Barrow J [S6]; in Barrow J there was a crushed red urn [S6]; the urn was decorated with cord impressions [S6]; the urn was inverted over burned bone [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ponton Heath Barrow Cemetery is a cemetery of round barrows (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The barrows lie south of Grantham [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The barrows lie on high ground [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; There is a Barrow J in the cemetery that held an urn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The urn was crushed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The urn was red [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The urn was decorated with cord impressions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The urn sat inverted (upside down) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]; The urn covered burned bone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponton_Heath_Barrow_Cemetery]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('353aa183-ed5a-4fa8-8894-3f3ff922cb58'::uuid, 'card_description', '1st century AD Roman military site near Honiton -- fort or marching camp. Timber-and-earth rampart, two deep ditches with ankle-breaker bases, and biological evidence of a cavalry garrison.', 'Fort or marching camp? Near Honiton in Devon, Pomeroy Wood held a Roman garrison with cavalry behind an earth and timber rampart and two deep ditches with narrow ankle breakers.', 'teaser-card-s012:353aa183-ed5a-4fa8-8894-3f3ff922cb58:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 177 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'f57961658e0459bb56cfdaa9b81cf0d8077f133756ad725a5c6268c029d7f19f|ef583ad31a570f271acc38d1f2e0a09c127b64b48bca4abcacebe9b93678f604|ef583ad31a570f271acc38d1f2e0a09c127b64b48bca4abcacebe9b93678f604', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fort or marching camp? Near Honiton in Devon, Pomeroy Wood held a Roman garrison with cavalry behind an earth and timber rampart and two deep ditches with narrow ankle breakers."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the question implies it is unknown whether it was a fort or a marching camp [S1]; it lies near Honiton [S1]; it lies in Devon [S1]; it was a Roman military site with a garrison [S1, S4]; the garrison included cavalry [S4]; it had an earth and timber rampart [S3]; it had two deep ditches outside the rampart [S3]; the ditches had narrow ankle breakers [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Pomeroy Wood is a Roman military site (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; It lies near Honiton [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; It lies in Devon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; It is unresolved whether it was a fort or a marching camp [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; It held a Roman garrison [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; The garrison included cavalry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; It had a rampart made of earth and timber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; Outside the rampart were two deep ditches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]; The ditches had narrow ankle breakers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomeroy_Wood]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('36408f22-774c-4626-a92f-e0c231f69206'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Rumicolca is situated near the archaeological sites Chuqi Pukyu and Pikillaqta, east of Lake Huacarpay.', 'Its name possibly comes from the Quechua words for stone and storehouse: Rumicolca, an archaeological site in the Cusco Region, lies east of Lake Huacarpay near Pikillaqta.', 'teaser-card-s012:36408f22-774c-4626-a92f-e0c231f69206:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 172 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'f6e3a47350be267ac0be31b8e55642e376d70e1cecdcaa52530715b7d42202de|7810727d8a046df95eb128bd81bf6c822926c766b705461b49f71bddeaaccc80|7810727d8a046df95eb128bd81bf6c822926c766b705461b49f71bddeaaccc80', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its name possibly comes from the Quechua words for stone and storehouse: Rumicolca, an archaeological site in the Cusco Region, lies east of Lake Huacarpay near Pikillaqta."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "its name possibly comes from the Quechua words for stone and storehouse [S1]; Rumicolca is an archaeological site [S1]; it is in the Cusco Region [S2]; it lies east of Lake Huacarpay [S3]; it lies near Pikillaqta [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rumicolca is an archaeological site (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; It is in the Cusco Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; The name possibly comes from Quechua words (the etymology is uncertain) [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; The Quechua word rumi means stone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; The Quechua word qullqa means storehouse [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; It lies east of Lake Huacarpay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]; It lies near Pikillaqta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumicolca]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('37d877c3-c4ce-4c4d-8326-1e249306a0b2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Set on a hilltop with the River Culm curving around a flat northern promontory, this Devon fort had water on three sides forming a natural moat. The hill rises to 128 meters at its peak.', 'Some 128 metres above sea level, the Iron Age hill fort of Dolbury holds a hilltop in Killerton Park, Devon, where a flat promontory juts north and the River Culm bends around it.', 'teaser-card-s012:37d877c3-c4ce-4c4d-8326-1e249306a0b2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 179 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5994fd4f23d92c7b6b6f487f6992f9404747b0ead0b59b015605b08fc25a6926|e3a06d4f8b9db7c43492771713cbe864223017c088836847568a47f0163c5013|e3a06d4f8b9db7c43492771713cbe864223017c088836847568a47f0163c5013', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Some 128 metres above sea level, the Iron Age hill fort of Dolbury holds a hilltop in Killerton Park, Devon, where a flat promontory juts north and the River Culm bends around it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the hilltop is some 128 metres above sea level [S2]; Dolbury is an Iron Age hill fort [S1, S2]; it occupies a hilltop [S2]; it is in Killerton Park [S1]; it is in Devon [S1]; a flat promontory juts out northwards [S2]; the River Culm bends around the promontory [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dolbury is an Iron Age hill fort (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; It is in Killerton Park [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; It is in Devon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; It holds a hilltop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; The hilltop is some 128 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; A flat promontory juts out to the north [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]; The River Culm bends around the promontory [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolbury]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('38325c7e-7878-4dc9-b9eb-318ac744d3c2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Up to 5,000 Neolithic tombs were once scattered across this region; over 1,000 survive today. The Funnelbeaker people built them in varying styles that differ from district to district.', 'The Megaliths in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern were raised as Neolithic tombs between 3,500 and 3,200 BC. Later, the Globular Amphora culture cleared out old skeletons to bury its own dead.', 'teaser-card-s012:38325c7e-7878-4dc9-b9eb-318ac744d3c2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'bad40f0bc4d9a89f1c411084aaa2488c79b90568acdc0bfe05d92f058b78255d|e74769ddaf4370e6d4deceab03d8d7f562e4d1801ff1a26a1bd359a5639d4126|e74769ddaf4370e6d4deceab03d8d7f562e4d1801ff1a26a1bd359a5639d4126', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Megaliths in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern were raised as Neolithic tombs between 3,500 and 3,200 BC. Later, the Globular Amphora culture cleared out old skeletons to bury its own dead."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the megaliths were erected as tombs [S1]; they date to the Neolithic [S1]; they were built between 3,500 and 3,200 BC [S1]; the Globular Amphora culture later reused the tombs [S5, S6]; the Globular Amphora culture removed skeletons of previous burials [S6]; the Globular Amphora culture buried its own dead in the tombs [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Megaliths in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern are megalith tombs (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]; The tombs are Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]; They were raised between 3,500 and 3,200 BC [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]; The Globular Amphora culture used the tombs later (subsequent to the tomb-builders) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]; The Globular Amphora culture cleared out old skeletons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]; The Globular Amphora culture did this to bury its own dead in the tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megaliths_in_Mecklenburg-Vorpommern]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('384133aa-dc2d-4f86-9f20-e53c98e5c5be'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The dam was first noted by Father Carvalho Costa in 1712 in his work, Corographia Portugueza.', 'First noted by Father Carvalho Costa in 1712, the Roman Dam of Pego da Moura in Grândola is a small buttress dam built almost entirely of opus incertum, braced by six downstream buttresses.', 'teaser-card-s012:384133aa-dc2d-4f86-9f20-e53c98e5c5be:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1e877430a69b1cb008cdf7fda1748673bd103ba074b2c8cd31148c39ce366ee8|e7d7020ca7f97c53db820cc73f446ae4d9e71e4091de95351a71d6b57485f9b2|e7d7020ca7f97c53db820cc73f446ae4d9e71e4091de95351a71d6b57485f9b2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "First noted by Father Carvalho Costa in 1712, the Roman Dam of Pego da Moura in Grândola is a small buttress dam built almost entirely of opus incertum, braced by six downstream buttresses."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was first noted by Father Carvalho Costa [S7]; it was first noted in 1712 [S7]; it is a Roman dam [S1, S2]; it is in Grândola [S1]; it is a small buttress dam [S1]; it was built almost entirely of opus incertum [S6]; it has six downstream buttresses [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-018 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Dam of Pego da Moura is a dam (central claim: a dam, a small buttress dam) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; It is located in Grândola (Portugal) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; The dam is Roman (built during the Roman occupation) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; The dam is small [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; It was first noted by Father Carvalho Costa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; The dam was first noted in 1712 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; The dam is built of opus incertum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; It is built almost entirely (not wholly) of opus incertum; the middle vaulted chamber uses opus signinum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]; The dam has six downstream buttresses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Pego_da_Moura]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3858c6d1-29e2-4bfa-99b5-cf015969646e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Ionic capitals of the Jandial temple seem to be a rather provincial and dry version of the Ionic Temple of Artemis in Ephesus.', 'Near Taxila, Jandial holds a temple called the most Hellenic structure yet found on Pakistani soil: at its front, two Ionic columns framed by two anta walls, as in a Greek layout.', 'teaser-card-s012:3858c6d1-29e2-4bfa-99b5-cf015969646e:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 179 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'dbc8c1ea9a54a1cf1c5e80fa38a86a9d271155c1af3a39446b3ac150cbf1ff6c|542127eb9ef6a34834857932c2f7eef44c9b54ff8dc63e2bad118e68696b8736|542127eb9ef6a34834857932c2f7eef44c9b54ff8dc63e2bad118e68696b8736', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Taxila, Jandial holds a temple called the most Hellenic structure yet found on Pakistani soil: at its front, two Ionic columns framed by two anta walls, as in a Greek layout."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Jandial is near Taxila [S1]; Jandial is the site of a temple [S1]; the temple has been called the most Hellenic structure yet found on Pakistani soil [S4]; at its front are two Ionic columns [S5]; the columns are framed by two anta walls [S5]; the arrangement follows a Greek layout [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-018 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Jandial holds (is the site of) an ancient temple (central claim: a temple) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; Jandial lies near Taxila [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; The temple has been called the most Hellenic structure yet found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; The site is on Pakistani soil (Pakistan) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; At its front stand two Ionic columns [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; The two columns are framed by two anta walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]; The layout is Greek (a Greek distyle in antis layout) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jandial]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3867504c-c199-4a59-9609-9b2340905467'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1933, schoolteacher Walenty Szwajcer and his students discovered remains of a Bronze Age fort/settlement in Wielkopolska Region.', 'Found in 1933 by a schoolteacher and his students, Biskupin is a late Bronze Age fortified settlement once taken for West Slavic, later confirmed as Lusatian culture of the 8th century BC.', 'teaser-card-s012:3867504c-c199-4a59-9609-9b2340905467:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '1377328244e067283befe9e570c0d7a0d491a0d27d1f10d64803fdb7077f5954|be7e542682c0a6cc07809240fac713993d12f1459d6790987c019dc465d3e755|be7e542682c0a6cc07809240fac713993d12f1459d6790987c019dc465d3e755', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Found in 1933 by a schoolteacher and his students, Biskupin is a late Bronze Age fortified settlement once taken for West Slavic, later confirmed as Lusatian culture of the 8th century BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was found in 1933 [S3]; it was found by a schoolteacher and his students [S3]; Biskupin is a late Bronze Age settlement [S1, S5]; the settlement was fortified [S1, S3]; it was at first thought to be a West Slavic settlement [S2]; archaeologists later confirmed it belonged to the Lusatian culture [S2]; it dates to the 8th century BC [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-018 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Biskupin is a fortified settlement (central claim: a fortified settlement) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; The settlement dates to the late Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; It was found in 1933 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; It was found by a schoolteacher [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; The schoolteacher''s students took part in the discovery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; It was once taken for (thought to be) West Slavic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; It was later confirmed as belonging to the Lusatian culture [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]; The Lusatian culture attribution is to the 8th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biskupin]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3963cd3e-5203-4506-a8a5-785aea857b71'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An oval stone circle of about 40 large pink granite stones, every single one now fallen. One lone limestone block sits among the granite — deliberately chosen to be different.', 'At the foot of Knott Hill in Cumbria, around 40 large stones of the Gamelands Stone Circle sit in a slight bank, an oval of 42 metres by 35 metres. Ploughing turned up two worked flints.', 'teaser-card-s012:3963cd3e-5203-4506-a8a5-785aea857b71:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '9184f901386e32b29566fd0f45713d342eb4ec294728c163e3f5ef0954059d9a|ec313f380ba5659686f166aac39358a9970a4e3efa8ef31c02392f5c5d8e8dbd|ec313f380ba5659686f166aac39358a9970a4e3efa8ef31c02392f5c5d8e8dbd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At the foot of Knott Hill in Cumbria, around 40 large stones of the Gamelands Stone Circle sit in a slight bank, an oval of 42 metres by 35 metres. Ploughing turned up two worked flints."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it lies at the foot of Knott Hill [S1]; it is in Cumbria [S1]; it is a stone circle [S1]; it has around 40 large stones [S3]; the stones are set into a slight bank [S4]; the stones form an oval of 42 metres by 35 metres [S5]; ploughing uncovered two worked flints [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-018 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gamelands Stone Circle is a stone circle (central claim: a stone circle in Cumbria, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; It lies at the foot of Knott Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; It is in Cumbria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; It consists of around 40 large stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; The stones sit in a slight bank [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; The circle is an oval [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; The oval measures 42 metres by 35 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; Ploughing turned up finds at the circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]; The finds were two worked flints [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamelands_stone_circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('39dd0b66-f565-4698-934f-37da9ff624dd'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'On the lower slope of Plačkovica Mountain lies Bargala, a city with a name of Thracian origin, seat of a bishopric in the 4th and 5th centuries with a basilica complex at its heart.', 'teaser-card-s012:39dd0b66-f565-4698-934f-37da9ff624dd:card_description', 'teaser-card-s012 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '76ba17fdaed6e28f278ebc762209aa3ef968b0454e36a7e84db5a8ebe02a26c5|dd3b5e2cb333c258e6341c81085d4333a1d5bed137d392d3c133a527749b66b4|dd3b5e2cb333c258e6341c81085d4333a1d5bed137d392d3c133a527749b66b4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the lower slope of Plačkovica Mountain lies Bargala, a city with a name of Thracian origin, seat of a bishopric in the 4th and 5th centuries with a basilica complex at its heart."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it lies on the lower slope of Plačkovica Mountain [S2]; Bargala was a city [S5]; its name is of Thracian origin [S3]; it was the seat of a bishopric in the 4th and 5th centuries [S6]; a basilica complex stood at its centre [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-018 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bargala was a city (central claim: an ancient city, archaeological site in North Macedonia) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bargala]; Bargala lies on the lower slope of Plačkovica Mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bargala]; The site''s name is of Thracian origin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bargala]; In the 4th and 5th centuries the city 